#{[#edb/ref 17592186048754 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065007} :activity/id #uuid "22bb5766-c513-5d78-a9f8-52a5e929a9f8" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065007 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065407 :tutorial/content "Remember that the exponential function $e^x$ has the special property that the derivative of the exponential function is the exponential function itself. In other words, taking the derivative doesn't change anything, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm d x} \\left(e^ x\\right)  = e^ x.\n$$\n\nSince integration is the opposite of differentiation, and differentiating the exponential function leaves it unchanged, integrating the exponential function also leaves it unchanged. So, the integral of the exponential function is\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int e^x \\, \\textrm d x = e^x +C.\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065008}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065008 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064171 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064049 :question/id #uuid "3ff03590-c618-5a60-b2ac-e0a20be6a8ae" :question/math-academy-id "e-377" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int 6e^x  \\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "Factoring out the constant $6$ and taking the integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\int 6e^x   \\textrm d x   =6 \\int e^x  \\, \\textrm d x =6e^x +C.\n                                                                               \n}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating the Exponential Function"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065009}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065009 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065408 :tutorial/content "Remember that if $a$ is a positive real number, then the derivative of $a^x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm d x}\\left( a^ x\\right)\t=\ta^ x \\ln a.\n$$\n\nSo, by \"reversing\" this process, we obtain the following formula for the integral of a general exponential function:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int  a^x \\, \\textrm d x  = \\dfrac{a^ x }{\\ln a} +C.\n$$\n\nFor instance, suppose that we wish to calculate the integral \n\n$$\n\\int 2^x  \\, \\textrm d x\n$$\n\nTo compute the integral of a general exponential function, we just need to divide by the natural logarithm of the base of the exponential function:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\int 2^x  \\, \\textrm d x   &= \\dfrac{2^x}{\\ln 2} + C\n}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Integrating an Exponential Function With a General Base"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065010}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065010 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064172 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064050 :question/id #uuid "66cbcaeb-93ee-5c15-a230-e66d2fbdd761" :question/math-academy-id "e-380" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int -6(7^x) \\, \\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor out the constant $-6.$ Then, we integrate the exponential function, and get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\int (-6)7^x  \\, \\textrm d x   \n&= -6\\int 7^x\\,\\textrm{d}x\\\n&=-6 \\cdot \\dfrac{7^x}{\\ln 7} + C.\n}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Exponential Function With a General Base"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065016}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065016 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064177 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064051 :question/id #uuid "90010ae5-e211-510f-b188-dc3006926d94" :question/math-academy-id "e-381" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int 5 \\cdot 9^x +3 e^x \\, \\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we combine the sum rule and our exponential rules to get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\displaystyle \\int 5 \\cdot 9^x +3 e^x  \\, \\textrm d x &= \\displaystyle 5\\int 9^x \\, \\textrm{d}x+ 3 \\displaystyle \\int e^x \\, \\textrm{d}x\\\n&= 5\\cdot \\dfrac{9^x}{\\ln 9} + 3 e^x +C.\n}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Sum of Exponential Functions"}}] :activity/title "Integrating Exponential Functions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064408}] [#edb/ref 17592186050771 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069612} :activity/id #uuid "b16543e4-9398-58b9-b3c3-d37c4e46b486" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069612 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070014 :tutorial/content "How can we solve an integral like\n\n$$\n\\int 2x(x^2+1)^3\\,\\textrm{d}x \\,?\n$$\n\nNotice that if we let $u=x^2+1$, then the derivative is $u' = 2x,$ and both $u$ and $u'$ are present in the integrand:\n\n$$\n\\int \\underbrace{2x}_{u'}(\\overbrace{x^2+1}^{u})^3\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nIntegrals like this can always be solved using substitution. So, let's proceed. If we let ${\\color{red}{u=x^2+1}},$ then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 2x\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\\color{blue}\\textrm{d}u=2x \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe can now see that the change of variables from $x$ to $u$ is convenient because if we substitute, we get\n\n$$\n\\int {\\color{blue}{2x}}({\\color{red}{x^2+1}})^3\\,{\\color{blue}{\\textrm{d}x}} =  \\int \\color{red}(x^2+1)^{\\color{black}3}\\, \\color{blue}{2x\\,\\textrm{d}x} \\color{black}= \\int {\\color{red}u}^{\\color{black}3}\\, {\\color{blue}{\\,\\textrm{d}u}} .\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the integral on the right since it's the integral of a power function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}^{3}\\,\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{{u}^{4}}{4}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the last step, we change the variable back to $x$ by replacing $u=x^2+1.$ So the final result is\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int 2x(x^2+1)^3\\,\\textrm{d}x \n=\\dfrac{(x^2+1)^4}{4}+C.\n$$\n\n**Note:** After solving an integral using substitution, we should always double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{({x}^{2}+1)^{4}}{4}+C] & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[({x}^{2}+1)^{4}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 4({x}^{2}+1)^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+1)+0 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)^{3}\\cdot (2x) \\\\  & =2x({x}^{2}+1)^{3}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069613}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069613 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068722 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068564 :question/id #uuid "e2579380-1933-5819-a39e-9ffcfdafeeee" :question/math-academy-id "e-452" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int 6x^2(2x^3+3)^6\\,\\textrm{d}x .$" :question/worked-solution "We note that $6x^2$ is the derivative of the quantity in the parentheses. So we make a substitution\n\n$$\nu=2x^3+3.\n$$\n\nIts derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =6{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=6{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand rewriting our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 6{x}^{2}(2{x}^{3}+3)^{6}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2{x}^{3}+3)^{6}\\,6{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {u}^{6}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve the integral using the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}^{6}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{{u}^{7}}{7}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we substitute $u=2x^3+3$ back in the expression. So the final result is\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int 6x^2(2x^3+3)^6\\,\\textrm{d}x =\\dfrac{(2x^3+3)^7}{7}+C.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Expression Involving a Power Function Multiplied by Its Derivative"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069614}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069614 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068723 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068565 :question/id #uuid "9bf7d343-8d3d-5194-93c4-7b80bc1e3609" :question/math-academy-id "e-453" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int x\\left(1+2x^2\\right)^6\\,\\textrm{d}x .$" :question/worked-solution "The $x$ that's outside the parentheses isn't quite the derivative of $(1+2x^2).$ The derivative of $(1+2x^2)$ is $4x,$ so we're out by a constant factor of $4$. However, since we're out only by a constant factor, substitution will still work!\n\nSo we make the substitution\n\n$$\nu=1+2x^2.\n$$\n\nIts derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =4x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u=x\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we can now write our integral in terms of $u,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{(1+2{x}^{2})}^{6}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {(1+2{x}^{2})}^{6}\\cdot x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {u}^{6}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int {u}^{6}\\text{d}u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve the integral in terms of $u$ and then write the final result in terms of $x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}\\int {u}^{6}\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{7}}{7}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{28}{u}^{7}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{28}{(1+2{x}^{2})}^{7}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Expression Involving a Power Function Multiplied by a Constant Multiple of Its Derivative"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069615}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069615 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070015 :tutorial/content "How do we calculate $\\displaystyle\\int \\dfrac{2x}{1+x^2}\\,\\textrm d x?$\n\nIn this case, the numerator is the derivative of the denominator! Again, integrals like this are always solvable by substitution. So, we let\n\n$$\n\\color{red}u = x^2+1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 2x\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad {\\color{blue}{\\textrm d u = 2x \\, \\textrm d x}},\n$$\n\nand we can now rewrite the integral in terms of $u$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\cdot 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve the integral in terms of $u$ and then write the final result in terms of $x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\operatorname{ln}|u|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}|1+{x}^{2}|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}(1+{x}^{2})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int \\dfrac{2x}{1+x^2}\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\ln(1+x^2)+C.\n$$\n\nNotice that we removed the absolute value bars because the function $1+x^2$ is always positive." :tutorial/title "Integrating Rational Functions Using Substitution"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069616}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069616 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068724 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068566 :question/id #uuid "49f679c4-bbc4-59a5-9b0c-0f3b69d7cf57" :question/math-academy-id "e-454" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{x^2}{x^3+1}\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=x^3+1.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 3x^2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 3 \\textrm d u= x^2 \\, \\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nand we can solve the resulting integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+1}\\cdot {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{3}+1|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Rational Function Using Natural Logarithms"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069706}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069706 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068786 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068628 :question/id #uuid "0461f8ba-c3c5-5e22-a679-32ee4f2a01b8" :question/math-academy-id "e-6149" :question/problem "$\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{x^2}{\\sqrt{1-x^3}} \\, \\textrm{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u = 1 - x^3.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = -3x^2 \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad -\\dfrac{1}{3} \\textrm d u= x^2 \\, \\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nand we can solve the resulting integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{3}}}\\cdot {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}(2\\sqrt{u})+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{u}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{1-{x}^{3}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Other Fractional Expressions"}}] :activity/title "Integration Using Substitution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068971}] [#edb/ref 17592186050473 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176635} :activity/id #uuid "a22648a9-1107-5fad-945a-32867ab1ed64" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176635 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177095 :tutorial/content "To find the slope of a line that is perpendicular to a given line $y=mx+b,$ we can just take the **negative reciprocal** of the given slope $m.$ So, the slope of the perpendicular line would be $-\\dfrac{1}{m}.$\n\nFor example, consider the line $y = 3x + 1,$ which has a slope of $m=3.$ The negative reciprocal of $3$ is $-\\dfrac{1}{3}.$ So, any line with a slope of $-\\dfrac{1}{3}$ will be perpendicular to the given line, because\n\n$$\n3 \\cdot \\left( -\\dfrac{1}{3} \\right) = -1.\n$$\n\nTo illustrate, some lines with a slope of $-\\dfrac{1}{3}$ are plotted below. We can see that they are all perpendicular to the given line $y=3x+1.$\n\n![](images/e2/e2772aa0cbe3e736e1129b52c59d3a7c36759a0ccf7b7a6b409cce09b2b22497.png)" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176636}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176636 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175922 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175735 :question/id #uuid "ca9ba6cf-860f-5868-b91f-1dff84406f22" :question/math-academy-id "e-14357" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that is perpendicular to $y = 4x - 1$ and passes through the point $(-8,7).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $l_1$ denote the line $y = 4x - 1,$ and let $l_2$ denote the line we want to find.\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of $l_2$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of $l_1.$ Since $l_1$ has a slope of $4,$ the line $l_2$ must have a slope of $-\\dfrac{1}{4}.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ and pass through the point $(x_1,y_1)=(-8,7).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\ny - y_1 &= m(x-x_1) \\[5pt]\ny - 7 &= -\\dfrac{1}{4}(x-(-8)) \\[5pt]\ny - 7 &= -\\dfrac{1}{4}(x+8)\\[5pt]\ny - 7 &= -\\dfrac{1}{4}x - 2 \\[5pt]\ny &= -\\dfrac{1}{4}x + 5\n\t}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Perpendicular Line"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176637}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176637 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175923 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175737 :question/id #uuid "ca9ec8da-5f74-53dc-afa7-e6cc965fcc95" :question/math-academy-id "e-14363" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(3,4)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(0,1)$ and $(-2,4).$" :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes which are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope $m_1$ of the line passing through the points $(0,1)$ and $(-2,4)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-1}{-2-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm \\cdot \\left(-\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right) = -1 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad m = \\dfrac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(3,4).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-4 & =\\frac{2}{3}(x-3) \\\\ y-4 & =\\frac{2}{3}x-2 \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{3}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Line Perpendicular to a Line Through Two Points"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176638}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176638 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175924 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175741 :question/id #uuid "49cee7e9-29a5-5e1e-bc13-7dbabaaea405" :question/math-academy-id "e-14730" :question/problem "What is the equation of the line that passes through the point $(6,2)$ and is perpendicular to $3x-2y=1?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation $3x-2y=1$ in its slope-intercept form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-2y & =1 \\\\ -2y & =-3x+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of the new line should be the negative reciprocal of the slope of the given line $y=\\dfrac{3}{2}x - \\dfrac{1}{2}.$ The given line has a slope of $\\dfrac{3}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-\\dfrac{2}{3}.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=-\\dfrac{2}{3}$ and pass through the point $(x_1,y_1) = (6,2).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-2 & =-\\frac{2}{3}(x-6) \\\\ y-2 & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+4 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Line Perpendicular to a Line in Standard Form"}}] :activity/title "Finding Equations of Perpendicular Lines" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176113}] [#edb/ref 17592186048245 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185245} :activity/id #uuid "aa1811b2-1877-5406-b70c-b443bbb27d74" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185245 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185377 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to calculate $\\displaystyle \\int 2x e^{x^2}\\,\\textrm d x.$\n\nNotice that if we let $u = x^2,$ then both $u$ and its derivative $u'=2x$ appear in the integrand:\n\n$$\n\\int \\underbrace{2x}_{u'} e^{\\overbrace{x^2}^u}\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nIntegrals like this can always be solved using substitution.\n\nLet $u=x^2.$ Differentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d} u}{\\textrm{d}x } = 2x \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d u = 2x\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute our expressions into the integral and evaluate it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2x{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,{{2x\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{\\text{d}u} \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={e}^{u}+C \\\\  & ={e}^{{x}^{2}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185246}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185246 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184349 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183945 :question/id #uuid "3e9586ee-12d1-5dbb-81f6-0f0a4998e00a" :question/math-academy-id "e-14402" :question/problem "Calculate  $\\displaystyle{\\int x^2 e^{x^3+1}\\, \\textrm{d}x }.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's introduce the new variable $u = x^3+1.$ Differentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= 3x^2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\\dfrac 1 3 \\textrm{d}u = x^2 \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{{x}^{3}+1}\\cdot \\,{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{u}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{{x}^{3}+1}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Products Containing the Exponential Function"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185247}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185247 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184350 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183946 :question/id #uuid "ab4772a2-f980-5ad7-a377-34e34b95be83" :question/math-academy-id "e-14403" :question/problem "Calculate  $\\displaystyle \\int x^3\\,{3^{1-x^4}} \\, \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's introduce the new variable $u= 1-x^4.$ Differentiating gives \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{ \\textrm{d}u}{ \\textrm{d}x} = -4x^3\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x^3 \\, \\textrm{d}x = -\\dfrac{1}{4} \\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\int {x^3\\,{3^{1-x^4}} }\\, \\textrm{d}x \n&=\\int {{3^{1-x^4}}\\cdot x^3 }\\, \\textrm{d}x \\[5pt]\n&=  \\int {3^{u}} \\cdot \\left(-\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right) \\textrm{d}u\\[5pt]\n                                                     &= -\\dfrac{1}{4}\\int {3^{u}}  \\textrm{d}u\\[5pt]\n                                                     &=  -\\dfrac{1}{4}\\cdot\\dfrac{3^{u}}{\\ln 3} + C\\[5pt]\n                                                     &=   -\\dfrac{3^{1-x^4}}{4\\ln 3}+ C.\n}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Products Containing Other Exponential Functions"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185248}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185248 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184351 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183947 :question/id #uuid "70fd3433-e905-5643-8e55-0bdf774d781e" :question/math-academy-id "e-14404" :question/problem "Calculate  $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac {e^{2x}} {e^{2x} + 1} \\, \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the numerator is proportional to the derivative of the denominator. So, we introduce the new variable $u = e^{2x}+1.$ Differentiating gives \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= 2e^{2x}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\\, \\textrm{d}u = e^{2x} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\cdot {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{e}^{2x}+1|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2x}+1)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, in the last step, we used the fact that $e^{2x} + 1 > 0$ for all $x.$"} :knowledge-point/title "More Complex Cases"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185249}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185249 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185378 :tutorial/content "Let's evaluate the following definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\int_{0}^{3} xe^{x^{2}}\\, \\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nWe'll use the change of variable method, which comprises three steps:\n\n**Step1: ** Change of variable in the integrand.\n\nLet's introduce the new variable $u = x^2.$ Differentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{ \\textrm{d}x} = 2x \n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\\textrm{d}u = x \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Find the new limits of integration.\n\nWe create a table below to change our limits of integration from $x$ to $u{:}$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $9$ |\n\n**Step 3:** Evaluate the integral in the new variable $u.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}x{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\cdot x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{9}{e}^{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{9}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{u}\\,{|}_{0}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{9}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{9}-1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** Often, there are several ways in which we can evaluate an integral using a change of variable. Whatever we use depends on the particular form of the integrand. In this tutorial, we take care of the applications of the properties\n\\[ \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(e^x\\right) = e^x \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(a^x\\right) = a^x\\ln a\\]\nwhich generally leads to nice integrands after the change of variable." :tutorial/title "Definite Integrals"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185250}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185250 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184352 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183948 :question/id #uuid "a99e8d2c-1934-57a9-9a6a-578ec9230f6f" :question/math-academy-id "e-14407" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle{\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt {\\ln 4}} 16xe^{-2x^2}\\, \\textrm{d}x}$." :question/worked-solution "Let's introduce the new variable $u=-2x^2.$ Differentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=-4x\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n-4 \\, \\textrm{d}u=16x \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the new limits of integration:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\sqrt {\\ln 4}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $-2\\ln 4$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}}16x{e}^{-2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}}{e}^{-2{x}^{2}}\\cdot 16x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{e}^{u}\\cdot (-4\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{0}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-4{e}^{u}\\,{|}_{0}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4} \\\\  & =-4({e}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =-4(\\frac{1}{16}-1) \\\\  & =4(\\frac{15}{16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Definite Integrals"}}] :activity/title "Integrating Exponential Functions Using Substitution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186184369}]}
